{
  "wireMagic": "pb3",
  "pens": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wsWinStyles": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wpWinPositions": [ {
  
  } ],
  "events": [ {
    "tStartMs": 1580,
    "dDurationMs": 4390,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Sometimes the simplest questions have the\nmost amazing answers."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 5970,
    "dDurationMs": 4980,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Like how can trees be so tall?\nIt's a question that doesn't even seem"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 10950,
    "dDurationMs": 780,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "like it needs an answer."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 11730,
    "dDurationMs": 3600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Trees just are tall.\nSome of them are over 100 meters."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 15330,
    "dDurationMs": 1720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Why should there be a height limit?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 17050,
    "dDurationMs": 4750,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "I'll tell you why. Tress need to transport\nwater from their roots up into their topmost"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 21800,
    "dDurationMs": 3770,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "branches in order to survive. And that is\nno trivial task."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 25570,
    "dDurationMs": 4570,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "There is a limit to the height that water\ncan be sucked up a tube - it's 10 meters."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 30140,
    "dDurationMs": 4990,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If you suck on a long vertical straw, the\nwater will go no higher than 10 meters. At"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 35130,
    "dDurationMs": 2960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "this point there will be a perfect vacuum\nat the top of the straw and the water will"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 38090,
    "dDurationMs": 5700,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "start to boil spontaneously. For a tree to\nraise water 100 meters, it would have to create"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 43790,
    "dDurationMs": 2810,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a pressure difference of 10 atmospheres."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 46600,
    "dDurationMs": 2040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "How would trees do that?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 48640,
    "dDurationMs": 4540,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "When I posed this conundrum, a lot of people\nsaid the answer is transpiration. And that's"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 53180,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "when water evaporates from the leaf, pulling\nup the water molecules behind it. Now that's"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 57900,
    "dDurationMs": 4630,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "clearly a mechanism a tree can use to create\nsuction, but it doesn't help us overcome this"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 62530,
    "dDurationMs": 3180,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "10 meter limit.\nThe lowest the pressure can go is a pure vacuum,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 65710,
    "dDurationMs": 3880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "which I imagine is not happening inside of\ntree leaves, right?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 69590,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Right, Hank. So you might suspect that a tree\ndoes not contain continuous straw-like tubes"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 75110,
    "dDurationMs": 5750,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The tree effectively has valves in it. So\nyou don't have a column of water"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 80860,
    "dDurationMs": 4620,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This big tube that you're saying needs to\nbe full of water is actually made up of cells."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 85480,
    "dDurationMs": 3940,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Although these are good speculations, they\ndon't turn out to be correct."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 89420,
    "dDurationMs": 4610,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Scientists who study trees find that the xylem\ntubes that transport water do contain a continuous"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 94030,
    "dDurationMs": 4590,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "water column. So how else could the tree transport\nwater from the roots to the leaves?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 98620,
    "dDurationMs": 2080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "They don't suck, they don't use a vacuum."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 100700,
    "dDurationMs": 2180,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "OK, so how do they do it?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 102880,
    "dDurationMs": 4290,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Squeezing like a cow udder all the way up.\nThey have little tree muscles in there."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 107170,
    "dDurationMs": 3020,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Yeah.\nBesides being a giant waste of energy, all"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 110190,
    "dDurationMs": 3300,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of the cells that make up the xylem tubes\nare all dead."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 113490,
    "dDurationMs": 4080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "What about osmotic pressure? If there is more\nsolute in the roots than in the surrounding"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 117570,
    "dDurationMs": 4780,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "soil, water would be pushed up the tree. But\nsome trees live in mangroves, where the water"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 122350,
    "dDurationMs": 4490,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is so salty that osmotic pressure actually\nacts in the other direction so the tree needs"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 126840,
    "dDurationMs": 3130,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "additional pressure to suck water into the\ntree."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 129970,
    "dDurationMs": 5150,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Then it must be capillary action. The thinner\nthe tube, the higher the water can climb."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 135120,
    "dDurationMs": 5300,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But the tubes in a tree are too wide - at\n20-200 micrometers in diameter, water should"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 140420,
    "dDurationMs": 1890,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "rise less than a meter."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 142310,
    "dDurationMs": 2320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So how do trees do it?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 144630,
    "dDurationMs": 4780,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Well one of the assumptions we made is wrong:\nThe lowest the pressure can go is a pure vacuum"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 149410,
    "dDurationMs": 1070,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "pure vacuum\npure vacuum"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 150480,
    "dDurationMs": 4350,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In a gas, this is true. When you eliminate\nall of the gas molecules, the pressure is"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 154830,
    "dDurationMs": 2860,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "zero and you have a perfect vacuum."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 157690,
    "dDurationMs": 5530,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But in a liquid, you can go lower than 0 pressure\nand actually get negative pressures. In a"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 163220,
    "dDurationMs": 4440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "solid, we would think of this as tension.\nThis means that the molecules are pulling"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 167660,
    "dDurationMs": 2640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "on each other and their surroundings."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 170300,
    "dDurationMs": 4740,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "As the water evaporates from the pores of\nthe cell wall, they create immense negative"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 175040,
    "dDurationMs": 6180,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "pressures of -15 atmospheres in an average\ntree. Think about the air-water interface"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 181220,
    "dDurationMs": 5190,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "at the pore. There is one atmosphere of pressure\npushing in and negative 15 atmospheres of"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 186410,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "suction on the other side. So why doesn't\nthe meniscus break? Because the pores are"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 191770,
    "dDurationMs": 7000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "tiny, only 2-5 nanometres in diameter. At\nthis scale, water's high surface tension ensures"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 198930,
    "dDurationMs": 4330,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the air-water boundary can withstand huge\npressures without caving."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 203260,
    "dDurationMs": 5110,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "As you move down the tree, the pressure increases,\nup to atmospheric at the roots. So you can"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 208370,
    "dDurationMs": 3660,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "have a large pressure difference between the\ntop and bottom of the tree because the pressure"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 212030,
    "dDurationMs": 2270,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "at the top is so negative."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 214300,
    "dDurationMs": 4060,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But hang on, if the pressure near the top\nis negative 15 atmospheres, shouldn't the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 218360,
    "dDurationMs": 1690,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "water be boiling?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 220050,
    "dDurationMs": 2549,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Yes. Yes it should."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 222599,
    "dDurationMs": 5661,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But changing phase from liquid to gas requires\nactivation energy. And that can come in the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 228260,
    "dDurationMs": 3850,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "form of a nucleation site like a tiny air\nbubble. That's why it's so important that"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 232110,
    "dDurationMs": 5150,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the xylem tubes contain no air bubbles, and\nthey can do this because unlike a straw, they"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 237260,
    "dDurationMs": 4670,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "have been water-filled from the start. This\nway, water remains in the metastable liquid"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 241930,
    "dDurationMs": 2430,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "state when it really should be boiling."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 244360,
    "dDurationMs": 4010,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It's just like supercooled water remains liquid\nwhen it really should be ice. So you could"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 248370,
    "dDurationMs": 4970,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "say that the water in a tree is supersucked\nbecause it remains liquid at such negative"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 253340,
    "dDurationMs": 1580,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "pressures."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 254920,
    "dDurationMs": 4539,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And why are trees moving all this water up\nthe tree? I want you to make a guess, say"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 259459,
    "dDurationMs": 1850,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it out loud.\nFor photosynthesis?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 261309,
    "dDurationMs": 5771,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Actually, no. Less than 1% of the water is\nused in photosynthetic reactions. Any other"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 267080,
    "dDurationMs": 3859,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "ideas?\nOk what about growth? Well 5% of the water"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 270939,
    "dDurationMs": 3831,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is used to make new cells.\nWell, so then what happens to the other 95%"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 274770,
    "dDurationMs": 3729,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of the water?\nIt just evaporates."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 278499,
    "dDurationMs": 4510,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For each molecule of carbon dioxide a tree\ntakes in, it loses hundreds of water molecules"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 283009,
    "dDurationMs": 830,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of water."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 283839,
    "dDurationMs": 730,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Woah."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 284569,
    "dDurationMs": 4891,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Can you believe how amazing this is? Trees\ncreate huge negative pressures of 10's of"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 289460,
    "dDurationMs": 5400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "atmospheres, by evaporating water through\nnanoscale pores, sucking water up 100m, in"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 294860,
    "dDurationMs": 3799,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a state where it should be boiling but can't\nbecause of the perfect xylem tubes contain"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 298659,
    "dDurationMs": 4641,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "no air bubbles, just so that most of it can\nevaporate in the process of absorbing a couple"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 303300,
    "dDurationMs": 3679,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "molecules of carbon dioxide."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 306979,
    "dDurationMs": 7000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "I will never look at a tree the same way again."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 319849,
    "dDurationMs": 4490,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "I'd like to say a huge thank you to Hank,\nHenry and Professor Poliakoff for making on"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 324339,
    "dDurationMs": 4931,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "camera hypotheses. This is an essential part\nof the scientific process even if your hypothesis"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 329270,
    "dDurationMs": 3660,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "turns out to be wrong.\nAs Einstein said, \"a person who has never"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 332930,
    "dDurationMs": 3959,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "made a mistake has never tried anything new.\"\nI've always wondered what it would be like"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 336889,
    "dDurationMs": 4321,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to be on this side of a Veritasium video."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 341210,
    "dDurationMs": 3560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Now I'd be surprised if you weren't already\nsubscribed to these guys, but if you're not,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 344770,
    "dDurationMs": 3959,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "go click on these annotations and check out\ntheir channels. You may just learn something."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 348729,
    "dDurationMs": 3580,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "I'd also like to thank Professor John Sperry\nfrom the University of Utah. He walked me"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 352309,
    "dDurationMs": 3841,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "through all of this in an hour-long Skype\nconversation so I'm going to put a link to"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 356150,
    "dDurationMs": 3810,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "his website in the description.\nWe're looking at pressures here below atmospheric,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 359960,
    "dDurationMs": 3500,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is that right?\nThat's right. Below atmospheric. This is liquid"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 363460,
    "dDurationMs": 6459,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "pressure not gas pressure. So it's a common\nmisconception that oh, you can't have you"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 369919,
    "dDurationMs": 4131,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "know negative pressures because there's no\nmolecules left. You know, the definition of"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 374050,
    "dDurationMs": 6349,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "pure vacuum is zero molecules. That's for\na gas, ok. So just to be clear..."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 380399,
    "dDurationMs": 3220,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "I think this was one of my big problems in\nunderstanding this."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 383619,
    "dDurationMs": 3720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This video would have been impossible without\nCGP Grey. When I told him in London about"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 387339,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "this idea in London...\nAnd I felt like 'pssshhh mind just blown with"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 392379,
    "dDurationMs": 2100,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "this whole thing'\nHe said it was going to be really hard to"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 394479,
    "dDurationMs": 4460,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "explain and when he says it's hard to explain\nyou know things are going to be tough. So"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 398939,
    "dDurationMs": 2460,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "thank you for all your input to this script."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 401399,
    "dDurationMs": 4540,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And thank you for watching. Making this video\nhas been a real odyssey for me so thank you"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 405939,
    "dDurationMs": 4171,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "for joining me on this journey. I really appreciate\nall of your comments and if you haven't subscribed"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 410110,
    "dDurationMs": 4600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to the channel already you can click the annotation\nor click the link above and join me on my"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 414710,
    "dDurationMs": 1909,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "next scientific adventure."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 416619,
    "dDurationMs": 7000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "I made a video promising to make a video about\nthe answer to this. I proposed the problem"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 424429,
    "dDurationMs": 3420,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "like a couple months ago, and I was like \"subscribe\nto the channel and I'll give you the answer"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 427849,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "next week.\" Hahaha\nOh, the lies."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 434249,
    "dDurationMs": 2900,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Drive it at the right frequency.\nOh"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 437149,
    "dDurationMs": 4091,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Yes!!\nSuccess is frightening."
    } ]
  } ]
}
